text:
say why a variable being categorical does not mean it should be encoded;
group rare categories and measure what that does to the number of columns;
find missing values that are not recorded as missing, and explain which convention is the most dangerous;
tell a data-entry error from a genuine extreme, and treat them differently;
derive BMI from height and weight, and see errors in the inputs propagate into it.

quantify how much data you would lose by dropping every incomplete row;
fill missing values with a mean or a median, and measure what that does to the spread of the column;
say why the median is the safer default for a skewed variable;
explain what iterative imputation does differently, and what it assumes;
argue for leaving a value missing rather than inventing one.

put features measured on different scales onto a comparable one, three different ways, and say what each one assumes;
predict which scaler an extreme value will damage, and check your prediction;
treat the log and the square root as one family rather than two tricks;
say what a squared term buys a linear model and why the model is still linear;
count the columns a feature-engineering step adds, and say why the count matters.

distinguish a repair that is fixed from a step that is fitted, and say which of the two must wait until after the split;
assemble the cleaning, imputation, encoding and scaling of Exercise 2.3 — Cleaning and encoding–Exercise 2.5 — Scaling, transformation and features into one object in both languages;
measure, rather than assert, what three different leaks do to a reported error;
explain why one of those leaks changes nothing at all, and why that is not a reason to commit it.
